From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:28:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Run the SQLite and RDB backends as executors of
 one engine

The IndexedDB backend had two engines with the same logic: upstream's
SQLite engine (engines/sqlite.rs with its sea-query models) and the
fork's RDB twin (engines/ohos_rdb.rs on the SQL of engines/shared.rs),
plus a wrapper that picked one of them from a pref (engines/twin.rs) and
a host test that compared their two copies of the schema. Every change
to the SQLite engine had to be mirrored into the twin by hand, and the
coming work on transactions, indexes and cursors would have had to be
written twice.

Both are replaced by one IndexedDB-on-SQL layer over a small executor
interface, in the new indexeddb/backend/ directory:

- executor.rs: `SqlExecutor`, one connection to a database. It runs
  statements and queries with typed parameters (`SqlParam`) and rows
  (`SqlRow`), and begins (deferred or immediate), commits and rolls back
  a transaction. A transaction stays open across calls until it is
  committed or rolled back, so that a later change can make each
  IndexedDB transaction one SQL transaction on one connection.
  `SqlError` covers both executors and maps a full disk to
  QuotaExceededError as before. There is no separate prepare step: the
  SQLite executor caches prepared statements by their text, and the RDB
  NDK has no prepare call.
- sqlite.rs: the executor on rusqlite.
- rdb.rs: the executor on the RDB wrapper. A transaction is an RDB
  transaction handle, which has a connection of its own; statements
  outside one go to the store, through OH_Rdb_ExecuteV2 and
  OH_Rdb_ExecuteQueryV2 (API 18), which the wrapper now binds.
- twin.rs: with both backends compiled in, the
  storage_ohos_rdb_backend_enabled pref now chooses the executor of each
  database, where it chose the engine.
- schema.rs (Gecko's schema, as before), queries.rs (the IndexedDB
  operations on it) and engine.rs (`SqlEngine`, the `KvsEngine` that the
  manager runs on).

The behaviour is RDB's: each batch of requests runs in one storage
transaction, BEGIN DEFERRED for readonly batches and IMMEDIATE for the
others (RDB used DEFERRED for all). The replies of readwrite and
versionchange batches are sent once the transaction has ended, and a
write that fails in the database rolls the batch back and fails all its
requests, while a request that fails before writing, such as one for a
missing store, fails alone. For SQLite this replaces autocommit, where
each request was reported as soon as it ran. A readonly batch has
nothing to make durable, so it replies to each request as soon as it
ran. The manager thread's metadata calls run on a connection of their
own, each in its own transaction, and each batch runs on another
connection on the thread pool, as before; the SQLite executor applies
the per-connection pragmas to the batch connections too.

The batches of a database now run one at a time, in the order the
manager hands them over. Overlapping readonly transactions used to run
their batches in parallel, and with the longer setup of a batch (the
pragmas, BEGIN and COMMIT) the result of a later one overtook an earlier
one often enough for idbtransaction-oncomplete to fail 5 of 60 host
runs, against none at the base. The database actor of a later change
runs every request of a database in order anyway.

The SQLite executor refuses the statements that RDB's executor rejects:
execute runs only what RDB takes, by its first keyword, for DDL, INSERT,
UPDATE, DELETE, REPLACE or PRAGMA, and query only statements that don't
write. SQL that would only fail on a device then fails in the host tests
too.

The on-disk format doesn't change: the same files and tables. The key
encoding stays in engines/sqlite/encoding.rs, which is upstream's file
again. tests/schema_parity.rs no longer compares IndexedDB schemas,
since there is one; its client storage check stays. sea-query is no
longer used, but stays a dependency for now, since dropping it changes
Cargo.lock and deny.toml.

The engine unit tests of both backends are one suite now, which runs on
every executor compiled in, with new tests of the executor contract
(transactions that span calls and roll back, isolation from other
connections, parameters, changed-row counts, a full disk, the statements
RDB rejects) and of the batch rules. A fault-injecting executor drives
the batch runner through a failing begin, a statement that ends the
transaction and a failing commit, the tests check that every request
replies exactly once, and that batches reply in the order they were
handed over.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` with SQLite: 71 passed (56 before: the 9 SQLite engine tests are now 22 executor-generic ones, plus 3 that need no executor, and the IndexedDB schema_parity test is gone). `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` warning-free with `--features ohos-rdb-backend`, with `--no-default-features --features ohos-rdb-backend` and without features; host `cargo clippy -p servo-storage --lib --profile test` reports only a finding in encoding.rs that predates this. DAYU200 unit tests (`./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- [--no-default-features] --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`): RDB only 69 passed, both backends 100 passed; the one skip is the documented test_new_storage_threads_create_independent_groups. WPT IndexedDB/ (820 tests, 4,511 subtests) with the whole P0 series applied, passing subtests: host SpiderMonkey 3,224 (baseline b17fe190449: 3,223 and 3,223) and host QuickJS 3,229 (baseline 3,226 and 3,227), 8 processes; DAYU200, QuickJS + RDB checked-release, 2 processes, idle host: 3,222 (baseline 3,226 and 3,223). The unexpected results are tests that time out or flip in runs of the baseline build too: on the host idbfactory-origin-isolation, the idbcursor-request-source sharedworker variant and idbobjectstore-rename-store.any.worker.html, which times out 10 of 10 times alone with either build; on the board interleaved-cursors-large in all three globals (58 to 64 s against the 60 s limit with either build), the idbindex-rename, idbobjectstore-rename-store and abort-metadata-revert worker variants, file_support, and idbversionchangeevent.any.worker.html, which then passed 10 of 10 repeats alone, against 0 of 10 with the baseline build. idbtransaction-oncomplete, window and worker, `--repeat 30 --processes 4`: 60 of 60 pass, as at the baseline; with the batches of a database in parallel it failed 5 of 60, and with only the early readonly replies 6 of 60.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent designed the executor interface, wrote the engine, the executors and the tests, and measured them on the host and the DAYU200; after a review, a later agent session made readonly batches reply per request and the batches of a database run in order, made the SQLite executor refuse what RDB rejects, added the fault-injection tests and measured again)
